# XSH pthread_key_delete()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# A deleted key is never used again (undefined). Where several
# destructors run, their order is unspecified.

assertion  XSH.pthread_key_delete.001
anchor     functions/pthread_key_delete.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/pthread_key_delete.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       pthread_key_delete() returns 0 for a key that the calling thread
           and another live thread hold non-NULL values for, and for a key
           no thread has set.
test       t/001-delete.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_key_delete.002
anchor     functions/pthread_key_delete.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       pthread_key_delete() calls no destructor, although the calling
           thread and another live thread hold non-NULL values for the key.
test       t/002-no-dtor-on-delete.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_key_delete.003
anchor     functions/pthread_key_delete.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       After its key is deleted, a destructor is not called when a
           thread that held a non-NULL value for the key ends.
test       t/003-no-dtor-after.c

assertion  XSH.pthread_key_delete.004
anchor     functions/pthread_key_delete.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       A destructor can delete another key: pthread_key_delete() called
           there returns 0, and the deleted key's destructor is not called
           after that.
note       If the other key's destructor ran first (the order is
           unspecified), only the return value is checked.
test       t/004-in-destructor.c
